00:09:20,933 --> 00:09:24,919
session is still running on your own

275
00:09:22,968 --> 00:09:27,140
computer with everything on it.

276
00:09:24,919 --> 00:09:29,976
Ideally, you should run all of this where

277
00:09:27,140 --> 00:09:32,207
none of this exists at all.   This is exactly what the

278
00:09:29,976 --> 00:09:34,717
Cloud session provides, because there it

279
00:09:32,207 --> 00:09:37,202
gets its own machine with a fresh copy of

280
00:09:34,717 --> 00:09:39,366
our repository.  And it works even

281
00:09:37,202 --> 00:09:41,368
when we close the laptop, which is definitely

282
00:09:39,366 --> 00:09:43,378
necessary for long sessions that

283
00:09:41,368 --> 00:09:45,976
last for hours.

284
00:09:43,378 --> 00:09:48,461
In this case, this automatically solves

285
00:09:45,976 --> 00:09:51,689
the problem with the files, because they are not in Git,

286
00:09:48,461 --> 00:09:53,782
nor are my SSH keys or GitHub token.

287
00:09:51,689 --> 00:09:55,908
And if you still need an API key,

288
00:09:53,782 --> 00:09:58,100
you can add it as a network secret, and

289
00:09:55,908 --> 00:10:00,258
the system will say, "hey, I'm missing an API

290
00:09:58,100 --> 00:10:02,744
key during the session," so it can

291
00:10:00,258 --> 00:10:05,113
use it without sending the

292
00:10:02,744 --> 00:10:07,346
model itself.  By default, network access in a Cloud session

293
00:10:05,113 --> 00:10:09,750
is limited to

294
00:10:07,346 --> 00:10:12,115
resources such as package registries on GitHub.

295
00:10:09,750 --> 00:10:13,944
So you will have to explicitly add these domains

296
00:10:12,115 --> 00:10:16,565
to your environment so that Cloud can

297
00:10:13,944 --> 00:10:18,649
contact other hosts.

298
00:10:16,565 --> 00:10:20,799
So, a Cloud session allows you to run

299
00:10:18,649 --> 00:10:22,950
long-running processes even with your

300
00:10:20,799 --> 00:10:24,836
laptop closed, and it provides security

301
00:10:22,950 --> 00:10:27,702
because everything runs in its own

302
00:10:24,836 --> 00:10:30,318
environment.  It's worth remembering that while this

303
00:10:27,702 --> 00:10:31,809
machine is temporary, the data that leaves it is

304
00:10:30,318 --> 00:10:33,281
not.  For example, when a commit

305
00:10:31,809 --> 00:10:35,135
is sent or a message is

306
00:10:33,281 --> 00:10:36,728
sent through a connector.  So we

307
00:10:35,135 --> 00:10:38,275
still run this in automatic

308
00:10:36,728 --> 00:10:39,799
mode with the ban rules, just

309
00:10:38,275 --> 00:10:41,634
commit them to the repository so they're

310
00:10:39,799 --> 00:10:43,424
always with us.  This can prevent

311
00:10:41,634 --> 00:10:45,442
things like forced pushes or

312
00:10:43,424 --> 00:10:47,207
secrets getting into commits, and things like that

313
00:10:45,442 --> 00:10:49,270
.

314
00:10:47,207 --> 00:10:51,434
In addition to security, we also need to

315
00:10:49,270 --> 00:10:54,113
make sure that the Cloud is actually working

316
00:10:51,434 --> 00:10:56,651
on the right tasks.  So the next

317
00:10:54,113 --> 00:10:58,866
step is verification.  The great thing is that we

318
00:10:56,651 --> 00:11:01,347
work in a cycle—we were confident in the

319
00:10:58,866 --> 00:11:03,119
quality of the work because we had already seen every

320
00:11:01,347 --> 00:11:05,160
change and approved everything.

321
00:11:03,119 --> 00:11:07,141
But now, if Cloud runs for hours in the

322
00:11:05,160 --> 00:11:09,437
background, we don't have that anymore

323
00:11:07,141 --> 00:11:12,137
.  Um, and we're not actually going to

324
00:11:09,437 --> 00:11:14,173
reread every file at the end.  So

325
00:11:12,137 --> 00:11:16,459
, you know, let's say we ask you to

326
00:11:14,173 --> 00:11:18,263
migrate our authentication to a new

327
00:11:16,459 --> 00:11:20,422
API.  Cloud understands what we

328
00:11:18,263 --> 00:11:22,839
want perfectly, but it still has to

329
00:11:20,422 --> 00:11:23,577
make a lot of small decisions in the process

330
00:11:22,839 --> 00:11:25,797
.

331
00:11:23,577 --> 00:11:28,216
So with the same request: one run

332
00:11:25,797 --> 00:11:30,337
can migrate everything but leave the old

333
00:11:28,216 --> 00:11:32,656
API, another can migrate only for new

334
00:11:30,337 --> 00:11:34,684
logins, and a third can migrate but

335
00:11:32,656 --> 00:11:36,719
log everyone out.

336
00:11:34,684 --> 00:11:38,755
All of these options seem pretty

337
00:11:36,719 --> 00:11:40,790
logical, so it's worth setting up

338
00:11:38,755 --> 00:11:43,122
checkpoints so that Cloud can constantly

339
00:11:40,790 --> 00:11:44,815
check its results.  This can be

340
00:11:43,122 --> 00:11:46,854
anything that produces a "

341
00:11:44,815 --> 00:11:49,553
pass" or "fail" result.  Tests,

342
00:11:46,854 --> 00:11:51,674
build, screenshot—whatever you have.

343
00:11:49,553 --> 00:11:53,864
So now we just want to make sure

344
00:11:51,674 --> 00:11:56,018
that we check these checkpoints.  They are

345
00:11:53,864 --> 00:11:57,683
much faster to check than if we

346
00:11:56,018 --> 00:12:00,214
had to review all the changes

347
00:11:57,683 --> 00:12:01,983
at the end.  So that's quite reasonable.  If

348
00:12:00,214 --> 00:12:03,724
one of these launches still manages to get everyone

349
00:12:01,983 --> 00:12:05,813
logged out, it will fail the first

350
00:12:03,724 --> 00:12:08,162
test and will be forced to look for another

351
00:12:05,813 --> 00:12:10,057
way.  There are several ways to set this up

352
00:12:08,162 --> 00:12:11,813
.  The easiest way, of course, is to simply

353
00:12:10,057 --> 00:12:13,983
specify it in the request.  You describe

354
00:12:11,813 --> 00:12:16,047
the result and ask to break the task into

355
00:12:13,983 --> 00:12:17,835
subtasks, each with its own tests.

356
00:12:16,047 --> 00:12:19,678
I also like to ask him

357
00:12:17,835 --> 00:12:21,689
to show the actual output, because you didn't

358
00:12:19,678 --> 00:12:23,629
see it happen.  But again

359
00:12:21,689 --> 00:12:25,894
, the query does not guarantee that the model

360
00:12:23,629 --> 00:12:28,476
will do everything accurately.  Therefore, for tasks

361
00:12:25,894 --> 00:12:30,952
that need to be guaranteed to be completed, I

362
00:12:28,476 --> 00:12:33,813
use hooks—these are scripts that Cloud

363
00:12:30,952 --> 00:12:35,790
Code runs at a specific time.  They

364
00:12:33,813 --> 00:12:37,703
give you a lot of control because you

365
00:12:35,790 --> 00:12:39,939
decide when to run them, what

366
00:12:37,703 --> 00:12:42,058
to run, and what to check.  For example,

367
00:12:39,939 --> 00:12:44,047
every time he edits a file, we can

368
00:12:42,058 --> 00:12:45,892
run our linter.  Whenever he

369
00:12:44,047 --> 00:12:47,585
wants to mark a task as done, we

370
00:12:45,892 --> 00:12:49,351
can run tests for the changed

371
00:12:47,585 --> 00:12:51,545
section.  If he touched the

372
00:12:49,351 --> 00:12:53,221
authentication code, he must pass the

373
00:12:51,545 --> 00:12:55,014
authentication tests.  And when he wants

374
00:12:53,221 --> 00:12:56,927
to complete the entire session, we can just

375
00:12:55,014 --> 00:12:58,990
run the full set of tests.  And if

376
00:12:56,927 --> 00:13:01,162
one of them doesn't pass, he says, "Oh,

377
00:12:58,990 --> 00:13:03,366
it still doesn't work."  He goes back

378
00:13:01,162 --> 00:13:05,686
until everything works and fixes

379
00:13:03,366 --> 00:13:08,130
it.  Another caveat: when a test

380
00:13:05,686 --> 00:13:10,786
fails, most tools by

381
00:13:08,130 --> 00:13:12,663
default exit with code

382
00:13:10,786 --> 00:13:15,437
1, but with code 1, Cloud Code still

383
00:13:12,663 --> 00:13:17,477
marks the task as completed.

384
00:13:15,437 --> 00:13:19,709
So make sure your scripts

385
00:13:17,477 --> 00:13:21,782
exit with code 2, because that's what

386
00:13:19,709 --> 00:13:23,156
makes Claude go back and fix the

387
00:13:21,782 --> 00:13:25,683
error.   So

388
00:13:23,156 --> 00:13:28,894
now we have a session that I

389
00:13:25,683 --> 00:13:30,944
can totally leave unattended.

390
00:13:28,894 --> 00:13:33,149
Now we can run more of them,

391
00:13:30,944 --> 00:13:35,322
because each one has its own machine, so they

392
00:13:33,149 --> 00:13:39,382
don't run in the same folder, and we

393
00:13:35,322 --> 00:13:41,663
don't have to think about working trees anymore.

394
00:13:39,382 --> 00:13:44,145
But we still run each session

395
00:13:41,663 --> 00:13:46,510
independently and check each one.  And

396
00:13:44,145 --> 00:13:48,928
, to be honest, this is already a pretty good

397
00:13:46,510 --> 00:13:51,275
level.  We still have full control over

398
00:13:48,928 --> 00:13:53,770
what gets run and when we consider a

399
00:13:51,275 --> 00:13:56,240
task complete.

400
00:13:53,770 --> 00:13:58,779
But even here, we still initiate

401
00:13:56,240 --> 00:13:59,858
each session ourselves and remain involved in

402
00:13:58,779 --> 00:14:02,088
all of them.

403
00:13:59,858 --> 00:14:04,223
So I think the most important thing here is to

404
00:14:02,088 --> 00:14:06,326
set up CI, because all of these sessions

405
00:14:04,223 --> 00:14:08,460
eventually create PRs, and your CI performs

406
00:14:06,326 --> 00:14:10,404
the same checks for each of them,

407
00:14:08,460 --> 00:14:12,220
regardless of how the session was

408
00:14:10,404 --> 00:14:14,396
set up or what Claude decided to

409
00:14:12,220 --> 00:14:15,961
do in the process.  And if you enable

410
00:14:14,396 --> 00:14:17,781
auto-fixes like CI auto-fix,

411
00:14:15,961 --> 00:14:19,318
Claude will also be able to see when one of

412
00:14:17,781 --> 00:14:21,360
these checks fails and

413
00:14:19,318 --> 00:14:23,137
go back to fix it.  So you

414
00:14:21,360 --> 00:14:24,968
don't have to waste your time

415
00:14:23,137 --> 00:14:26,906
checking what an

416
00:14:24,968 --> 00:14:28,462
automated test could have already detected.  And for some of them,

417
00:14:26,906 --> 00:14:30,167
you can even enable the "

418
00:14:28,462 --> 00:14:32,062
merge when ready" feature.  GitHub then

419
00:14:30,167 --> 00:14:33,839
automatically merges the PR once all

420
00:14:32,062 --> 00:14:35,791
checks have passed.  So, you don't

421
00:14:33,839 --> 00:14:37,712
need to interfere with the verification process.

422
00:14:35,791 --> 00:14:39,166
But, again, this is not a standard, and it all

423
00:14:37,712 --> 00:14:41,431
depends on how

424
00:14:39,166 --> 00:14:43,782
risky the changes are.  For example, in our

425
00:14:41,431 --> 00:14:46,033
team, every PR is first reviewed by

426
00:14:43,782 --> 00:14:48,063
Claude.  That is, not the session that

427
00:14:46,033 --> 00:14:50,217
wrote it.  Therefore, it receives fresh

428
00:14:48,063 --> 00:14:52,038
context, as well as a separate group of agents

429
00:14:50,217 --> 00:14:54,120
to re-check each

430
00:14:52,038 --> 00:14:56,238
bug found.  And the extent to which we

431
00:14:54,120 --> 00:14:58,299
check everything ourselves depends solely on the

432
00:14:56,238 --> 00:15:00,274
risk.  Minor changes, such as documentation

433
00:14:58,299 --> 00:15:01,887
or texts, do not require human intervention.

434
00:15:00,274 --> 00:15:03,706
So, as soon as the automated checks

435
00:15:01,887 --> 00:15:05,790
give the "green light," we're ready to

436
00:15:03,706 --> 00:15:08,054
go.  Maybe regular product

437
00:15:05,790 --> 00:15:10,585
changes only require one reviewer

438
00:15:08,054 --> 00:15:13,051
, while risky ones require two.

439
00:15:10,585 --> 00:15:15,217
Ultimately, we see things developing in a way—and

440
00:15:13,051 --> 00:15:17,683
we at Anthropic are already doing this more often—that

441
00:15:15,217 --> 00:15:20,168
you don't really need to run all

442
00:15:17,683 --> 00:15:22,028
these sessions yourself.  Because if you're anything like

443
00:15:20,168 --> 00:15:23,933
me, I'm terrible at switching between

444
00:15:22,028 --> 00:15:25,971
contexts.  Therefore, managing so

445
00:15:23,933 --> 00:15:28,094
many sessions at the same time does not always

446
00:15:25,971 --> 00:15:29,956
work well.  So instead

447
00:15:28,094 --> 00:15:32,341
you have just one main session that

448
00:15:29,956 --> 00:15:34,585
acts as your orchestrator.  This

449
00:15:32,341 --> 00:15:36,692
can already be seen in projects like

450
00:15:34,585 --> 00:15:39,801
Claude Code, which is still in beta, where

451
00:15:36,692 --> 00:15:41,844
you just say what needs to be done.

452
00:15:39,801 --> 00:15:44,211
The orchestrator breaks this task down,

453
00:15:41,844 --> 00:15:46,621
launches separate sessions for each one,

454
00:15:44,211 --> 00:15:48,664
and tracks the results.  It automatically

455
00:15:46,621 --> 00:15:50,887
starts and checks these threads, which

456
00:15:48,664 --> 00:15:52,992
are really just new sessions.  So

457
00:15:50,887 --> 00:15:55,460
instead of talking to eight sessions,

458
00:15:52,992 --> 00:15:57,697
you talk to just one.  Moreover, the

459
00:15:55,460 --> 00:15:59,708
main session itself does not write code.  What

460
00:15:57,697 --> 00:16:01,893
you need is a model that is good at

461
00:15:59,708 --> 00:16:03,673
deciding what to do and evaluating the

462
00:16:01,893 --> 00:16:05,771
result, like Fable.

463
00:16:03,673 --> 00:16:07,899
And if the result is not complete, she

464
00:16:05,771 --> 00:16:10,117
simply sends it back until everything

465
00:16:07,899 --> 00:16:12,703
is ready and passes verification.  And

466
00:16:10,117 --> 00:16:15,003
for streams, you need a model that

467
00:16:12,703 --> 00:16:17,478
does the job well, like the Opus 55. You

468
00:16:15,003 --> 00:16:19,767
can configure them separately.  That is, one

469
00:16:17,478 --> 00:16:21,608
model for the main session, and another for

470
00:16:19,767 --> 00:16:23,342
streams.  Another advantage of this

471
00:16:21,608 --> 00:16:25,162
setup, particularly in

472
00:16:23,342 --> 00:16:27,375
CloudCode projects, is that we

473
00:16:25,162 --> 00:16:29,528
no longer need to manage the context.  This is all

474
00:16:27,375 --> 00:16:31,784
done automatically.

475
00:16:29,528 --> 00:16:34,623
We no longer need to think about

476
00:16:31,784 --> 00:16:36,523
when to compress or purge all of this.  Okay

477
00:16:34,623 --> 00:16:38,632
.  So, if you look back at the old

478
00:16:36,523 --> 00:16:40,712
best practices, they are all still there,

479
00:16:38,632 --> 00:16:43,117
but they just changed places.  Now

480
00:16:40,712 --> 00:16:45,205
the model drives the process, auto mode approves

481
00:16:43,117 --> 00:16:47,773
your ban rules, and the environment

482
00:16:45,205 --> 00:16:50,097
limits the possibilities.  Your tests, hooks, and

483
00:16:47,773 --> 00:16:52,502
CI all check this.  And eventually, the Cloud

484
00:16:50,097 --> 00:16:54,461
can even run them on its own.  So

485
00:16:52,502 --> 00:16:56,089
almost everything we used to do ourselves is

486
00:16:54,461 --> 00:16:58,388
now done by either a model, a

487
00:16:56,089 --> 00:17:00,618
toolkit, or an environment.  We are

488
00:16:58,388 --> 00:17:02,842
only here to launch the first session

489
00:17:00,618 --> 00:17:04,845
and review the result.  If you want to

490
00:17:02,842 --> 00:17:06,962
try this out for yourself, I would recommend taking on

491
00:17:04,845 --> 00:17:08,699
one task that is significantly larger than what

492
00:17:06,962 --> 00:17:10,251
you would typically assign to Cloud.

493
00:17:08,699 --> 00:17:12,246
Make sure your CI is actually

494
00:17:10,251 --> 00:17:14,020
catching the things that are important to you, and then

495
00:17:12,246 --> 00:17:15,765
spend some time setting up your

496
00:17:14,020 --> 00:17:17,234
environment, such as network

497
00:17:15,765 --> 00:17:19,717
access, secrets, and a

498
00:17:17,234 --> 00:17:21,932
setup script.  And then start with the cloud and

499
00:17:19,717 --> 00:17:24,055
see where your sessions are hanging and

500
00:17:21,932 --> 00:17:25,803
where you're still in the loop, because that's the next thing to

501
00:17:24,055 --> 00:17:27,732
move to the cloud.  I also

502
00:17:25,803 --> 00:17:29,285
highly recommend reviewing

503
00:17:27,732 --> 00:17:33,133
our best practices in the

504
00:17:29,285 --> 00:17:33,133
documentation.  Thank you very much.

